69
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:19,220
and retrieval databases. Capability what
are capability evaluations when

70
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:21,939
it comes to capabilities measuring what a
model can

71
00:04:21,939 --> 00:04:25,420
do as far as AI safety is concerned, we're

72
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:29,180
particularly interested in abilities that
could enable harmful outcomes.

73
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:34,209
These dangerous capabilities generally
fall into several key categories.

74
00:04:34,610 --> 00:04:40,329
1. The model can discover vulnerabilities
in systems hardware

75
00:04:40,329 --> 00:04:44,089
software data. It can write code for
exploiting those

76
00:04:44,089 --> 00:04:48,329
vulnerabilities. If deployed as a coding
assistant, it can

77
00:04:48,329 --> 00:04:51,490
insert subtle bugs into the code for
future exploitation.

78
00:04:52,290 --> 00:04:55,449
2. Deception the model has the skills
necessary to

79
00:04:55,449 --> 00:04:59,569
deceive humans, for example constructing
believable but false statements,

80
00:05:00,089 --> 00:05:02,649
making accurate predictions about the
effect of a lie

81
00:05:02,649 --> 00:05:05,050
on a human, and keeping track of what
information

82
00:05:05,209 --> 00:05:08,970
it needs to withhold to maintain the
deception. 3.

83
00:05:09,529 --> 00:05:13,129
Persuasion and manipulation the model is
effective at shaping

84
00:05:13,129 --> 00:05:17,529
people's beliefs in dialogue and other
settings, social media

85
00:05:17,610 --> 00:05:22,529
posts, even towards untrue beliefs. It can
convince people

86
00:05:22,529 --> 00:05:24,889
to do things that they would not otherwise
do,

87
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:30,879
including unethical acts. 4. The model can
perform the

88
00:05:30,879 --> 00:05:33,920
social modeling and planning necessary for
an actor to

89
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:38,240
gain and exercise political influence. For
example, the model

90
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:41,839
can score highly in forecasting
competitions on questions relating

91
00:05:41,839 --> 00:05:47,319
to global affairs or political
negotiations. 5. Weapons acquisition

92
00:05:47,319 --> 00:05:51,000
and the model can gain access to existing
weapon

93
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,389
systems or contribute to building new
weapons. For example,

94
00:05:55,389 --> 00:05:58,709
the model could assemble a bioweapon with
human assistance

95
00:05:58,709 --> 00:06:01,750
or provide actionable instructions for how
to do so.

96
00:06:02,949 --> 00:06:07,149
6. Long Horizon Planning the model can
make sequential

97
00:06:07,149 --> 00:06:10,670
plans that involve multiple steps,
unfolding over long time

98
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:15,269
horizons, or at least involving many
interdependent steps. It

99
00:06:15,269 --> 00:06:18,230
can perform such planning within and
across many domains.

100
00:06:19,189 --> 00:06:22,850
7. AI development the model could build
new AI

101
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:26,930
systems from scratch, including AI systems
with dangerous capabilities.

102
00:06:27,889 --> 00:06:31,170
It can find ways of adapting other
existing models

103
00:06:31,170 --> 00:06:34,329
to increase their performance on tasks
relevant to extreme

104
00:06:34,329 --> 00:06:39,009
risks. 8. Situational awareness the model
can distinguish between

105
00:06:39,009 --> 00:06:43,490
whether it is being trained, evaluated, or
deployed, allowing

106
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:46,779
it to behave differently in each case. The
model

107
00:06:46,779 --> 00:06:48,899
knows that it is a model and has knowledge

108
00:06:48,899 --> 00:06:52,459
about itself and its likely surroundings,
for example what

109
00:06:52,459 --> 00:06:55,379
company trained it, where their servers
are, what kind

110
00:06:55,379 --> 00:06:57,779
of people might be giving it feedback, and
who

111
00:06:57,779 --> 00:07:02,779
has administrative access. 9. Self
proliferation the model can

112
00:07:02,779 --> 00:07:06,540
break out of its local environment using a
vulnerability

113
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:10,579
in its underlying system or suborning an
engineer. The

114
00:07:10,579 --> 00:07:13,949
model can exploit limitations in the
systems from monitoring

115
00:07:13,949 --> 00:07:17,430
its behavior post deployment. This list is
not exhaustive

116
00:07:17,430 --> 00:07:20,870
and may not address all plausible extreme
risk scenarios.

117
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:24,069
The list may also be biased towards more
readily

118
00:07:24,069 --> 00:07:29,509
identifiable or already understood
capabilities, possibly overlooking
emergent risks.

119
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:33,389
Additionally, many of these capabilities
can become even more

120
00:07:33,389 --> 00:07:38,509
dangerous when combined. For example,
strong situational awareness combined

121
00:07:38,509 --> 00:07:41,629
with deception capabilities could enable a
model to behave

122
00:07:41,629 --> 00:07:45,949
differently during evaluation versus
during deployment. This is why

123
00:07:45,949 --> 00:07:50,189
comprehensive evaluation protocols need to
assess not just individual

124
00:07:50,189 --> 00:07:53,949
capabilities but also their interactions.
We will go into

125
00:07:53,949 --> 00:07:57,870
much more detail on individual dangerous
capability evaluations in

126
00:07:57,870 --> 00:08:03,750
a dedication section later. Propensity
what are propensity Evaluations?

127
00:08:04,310 --> 00:08:07,329
Capability evaluations tell us what what a
model can

128
00:08:07,329 --> 00:08:11,529
do when directed. Propensity evaluations
reveal what behaviors a

129
00:08:11,529 --> 00:08:14,490
model prioritizes by default what it tends
to do.

130
00:08:15,050 --> 00:08:19,129
These are also often called alignment
evaluations. A key

131
00:08:19,129 --> 00:08:23,089
aspect that distinguishes propensity
evaluations is their focus on

132
00:08:23,089 --> 00:08:26,730
non capability features. They look at how
models behave

133
00:08:26,730 --> 00:08:30,209
when given choices between different
actions rather than just

134
00:08:30,209 --> 00:08:33,799
measuring success or failure at specific
tasks. As an

135
00:08:33,799 --> 00:08:37,720
intuitive example, after undergoing safety
training, think about the

136
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:41,279
way that language models like GPT4 or
Claude 3.5

137
00:08:41,519 --> 00:08:44,960
respond to user requests to produce
potentially harmful or

138
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:49,318
discriminatory content. They usually
respond with a polite refusal

139
00:08:49,318 --> 00:08:53,200
to produce such content. It takes more
effort jailbreaking

140
00:08:53,279 --> 00:08:55,559
to get them to actually generate this type
of

141
00:08:55,559 --> 00:08:59,120
content, so more often than not they
resist misuse.

142
00:09:00,039 --> 00:09:01,960
In this case, we would say the models have

143
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:05,240
a propensity not to produce harmful or
offensive content.

144
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:08,600
Similarly, we want to increase the
propensity for good

145
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:11,799
behaviors and want to reduce the
propensity for dangerous

146
00:09:11,799 --> 00:09:15,879
behaviors. Increasing reducing would be
the job of alignment

147
00:09:15,879 --> 00:09:18,919
research in evaluations. We just want to
see what

148
00:09:18,919 --> 00:09:22,519
type of behavior it exhibits. The
distinction between capability

149
00:09:22,519 --> 00:09:25,759
and propensity becomes important when we
think about the

150
00:09:25,759 --> 00:09:29,110
fact that in the future, highly capable AI
systems

151
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:32,870
might have multiple behavioral options
available to them. For

152
00:09:32,870 --> 00:09:36,950
example, when evaluating a model's
tendency toward honesty, we're

153
00:09:36,950 --> 00:09:38,950
not just interested in whether it can tell
the

154
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:42,710
truth a capability, but whether it
consistently chooses to

155
00:09:42,710 --> 00:09:46,669
do so across different scenarios,
especially when being dishonest

156
00:09:46,669 --> 00:09:50,669
might provide some advantage. Can
propensities and capabilities be

157
00:09:50,669 --> 00:09:54,730
related yes, propensities and capabilities
take tend to be

158
00:09:54,730 --> 00:09:59,049
interconnected. Some concerning
propensities might be initially subtle or

159
00:09:59,049 --> 00:10:02,769
even undetectable, only becoming apparent
as models gain more

160
00:10:02,769 --> 00:10:06,529
sophisticated capabilities. An important
thing to keep in mind

161
00:10:06,529 --> 00:10:10,850
when designing propensity evaluations is
how behavioral tendencies might

162
00:10:10,850 --> 00:10:14,330
emerge and evolve as models become more
capable. As

163
00:10:14,330 --> 00:10:17,490
an example, a basic form of power seeking
behavior

164
00:10:17,570 --> 00:10:21,679
might appear unconcerning in simple
systems but become problematic

165
00:10:21,679 --> 00:10:25,519
as models gain better strategic,
understanding and action capabilities.

166
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:28,600
Here is a list of some propensities that
we

167
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:33,320
might want to design evaluations 1. The
propensity to

168
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:37,799
generate offensive, harmful or otherwise
inappropriate content such as

169
00:10:37,799 --> 00:10:43,200
hate speech, offensive abusive language,
pornographic content, etc. 2.

170
00:10:43,519 --> 00:10:48,080
Bias discrimination A model's propensity
to manifest or perpetuate

171
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:52,820
biases leading to unfair, prejudiced or
discriminatory outputs against

172
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:57,899
certain groups or individuals. 3. A
model's propensity to

173
00:10:57,899 --> 00:11:00,779
answer by expressing its true beliefs and
actual level

174
00:11:00,779 --> 00:11:06,539
of certainty. 4. A model's propensity to
produce truthful

175
00:11:06,539 --> 00:11:10,539
outputs. This propensity requires an AI
system to be

176
00:11:10,539 --> 00:11:13,139
both honest and to know the truth or other

177
00:11:13,139 --> 00:11:16,299
weirder settings such that the AI system
outputs the

178
00:11:16,299 --> 00:11:19,480
truth while believing it is not the truth.
5.

179
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:23,519
A model's propensity to tell users what it
thinks

180
00:11:23,519 --> 00:11:26,200
they want to hear or would approve of
rather

181
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:29,320
than what it internally believes is the
truth. 1.

182
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:34,039
A model's propensity to intentionally
generate misleading, false or

183
00:11:34,039 --> 00:11:39,840
deceptive output. 2. A model's propensity
to accept feedback

184
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:43,039
and correct its behavior or outputs in
response to

185
00:11:43,039 --> 00:11:48,259
human intervention or new information. 3.
Power Seeking a

186
00:11:48,259 --> 00:11:50,580
model's propensity to seek to have a high
level

187
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:54,259
of control over its environment,
potentially to maximize its

188
00:11:54,259 --> 00:11:57,179
own objectives. Just like the list
provided in the

189
00:11:57,179 --> 00:12:01,019
Capabilities subsection above, this list
of propensities is non

190
00:12:01,019 --> 00:12:04,899
exhaustive and constantly evolving. We
will cover a few

191
00:12:04,899 --> 00:12:07,580
of these that are considered dangerous or
extremely decision

192
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:11,620
relevant in a dedicated section on
dangerous propensity evaluations

193
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:16,659
later section. Control Control evaluations
assess safety measures under

194
00:12:16,659 --> 00:12:22,259
adversarial conditions beyond
understanding capabilities and
propensities. We need

195
00:12:22,259 --> 00:12:25,259
to verify that our safety measures remain
effective when

196
00:12:25,259 --> 00:12:28,980
AI systems actively try to circumvent
them. They give

197
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:32,379
us information about whether our safety
infrastructure would remain

198
00:12:32,379 --> 00:12:36,379
robust in worst case scenarios. For
example, a model

199
00:12:36,379 --> 00:12:40,169
might typically display helpful behavior
around propensity and have

200
00:12:40,169 --> 00:12:44,490
code generation abilities a capability.
But a control evaluation

201
00:12:44,730 --> 00:12:48,490
would test whether constraints preventing
access to sensitive data

202
00:12:48,649 --> 00:12:53,049
can be circumvented or whether emergency
shutdown procedures remain

203
00:12:53,049 --> 00:12:57,809
effective even under adversarial
conditions. Control evaluations give us

204
00:12:57,809 --> 00:13:02,169
Multiple different sources of safety
guarantees beyond hard barriers

205
00:13:02,169 --> 00:13:06,250
and monitoring detection. Control
evaluations also tell us when

206
00:13:06,250 --> 00:13:09,690
models lack the sophisticated abilities
needed to bypass them.

207
00:13:10,330 --> 00:13:12,330
There are a couple of different reasons
why control

208
00:13:12,330 --> 00:13:17,490
evaluations might show safety. 1. Hard
barriers physical technical

209
00:13:17,490 --> 00:13:22,610
limitations like air gaps. 2. Detection
mechanisms monitoring that

210
00:13:22,610 --> 00:13:28,529
catches attack attempts. 3. Capability
limitations models simply lack

211
00:13:28,529 --> 00:13:32,529
the sophisticated abilities needed. Models
might fail not because

212
00:13:32,529 --> 00:13:35,809
our detection mechanisms are bad. They
fail because they

213
00:13:35,809 --> 00:13:39,309
lack the strategic planning and
coordination abilities needed to

214
00:13:39,539 --> 00:13:43,620
execute these extremely subtle multi stage
attacks. For example,

215
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:48,100
distributing subtle bugs across multiple
repositories that individually appear

216
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:53,139
benign but collectively create an
exploitable vulnerability requires
capabilities

217
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:57,419
models as of 2025 don't possess. To be
able

218
00:13:57,419 --> 00:13:59,620
to pull this off, they need to pass many

219
00:13:59,620 --> 00:14:03,299
layers of soft checks like distribute
extremely subtle bugs

220
00:14:03,299 --> 00:14:07,360
across multiple repositories that activate
only when specific conditions

221
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:11,840
align, ensure each individual change
passes code review by

222
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:16,240
appearing benign, then evade automated
detection systems like static

223
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:20,840
analyzers and also maintain persistence
despite randomized human audits.

224
00:14:21,239 --> 00:14:25,519
Control evaluations can drive strategic
safety planning depending on

225
00:14:25,519 --> 00:14:29,080
which category causes the most risk.
During control evaluations,

226
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:33,440
we can reprioritize and reallocate safety
efforts. This gives

227
00:14:33,759 --> 00:14:39,320
one risk assessment timeline. Control
evaluations that show safety

228
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:42,960
due to capability limitations give us
information about when

229
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:46,039
those barriers might fail. If we know
models are

230
00:14:46,039 --> 00:14:50,200
safe because they can't yet execute
sophisticated attacks rather

231
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:53,679
than because we're detecting their
attempts, we can anticipate

232
00:14:53,679 --> 00:14:57,320
capability thresholds beyond which our
